\description{
An isoform-level result is only as good as the quantification behind it.
Reads that map to several isoforms of a gene can be shared out between
them in more than one way, so the estimate of each isoform carries an
\emph{inferential} uncertainty that is unrelated to biological variability.
Salmon measures it by drawing replicates from the posterior (Gibbs
sampling, \code{--numGibbsSamples}) and by writing the equivalence classes
(\code{--dumpEq}). These functions turn that information into columns that can
be filtered on and reported.

Every isoform gets four kinds of evidence:
\itemize{
\item \strong{Quantification uncertainty}: the inferential relative variance
(\code{mean_inf_rv}, Zhu et al. 2019, as in \code{fishpond}), the variance of the
replicates in excess of what counting noise alone would give, relative
to the expression. Isoforms with a high value are not well determined by
the reads. \code{uncertain} flags \code{mean_inf_rv >= inf_rv_cutoff}.
\item \strong{Identifiability}: the correlation between the replicates of isoforms
of the same gene. A strong negative correlation means the reads cannot
tell the two isoforms apart and every read taken from one is given to
the other. Pairs at or below \code{cor_cutoff} are \emph{ambiguous} and are
joined into \code{ambiguity_group}s that can be tested as one feature.
\item \strong{Read support}: the share of the fragments of an isoform that map to
it alone (\code{unique_fraction}, from the equivalence classes).
\item \strong{Stability of the fold-change}: the contrast between two groups is
repeated on every replicate. \code{lfc_lo}/\code{lfc_hi} is the 95\% interval of
the log2 fold-change over replicates, \code{sign_agreement} the share of
replicates that agree on its direction and \code{frac_draws_sig} the share in
which the isoform is called differentially expressed.
}

\code{de_confidence()} combines the last three into one class per isoform:
\code{high} when the fold-change is stable (\code{sign_agreement >= min_agreement}),
the isoform is not \code{uncertain} and has no ambiguous sibling; \code{moderate}
when exactly one of these fails; \code{low} when two or more fail. The class
says how far a call on the isoform can be trusted, so read it for the
isoforms flagged by the primary test (\code{n_det_sig}, \code{confident_de}): an
isoform without a real change is \code{moderate} or \code{low} simply because its
fold-change is not stable.
\itemize{
\item \code{add_uncertainty(x, paths)} reads the replicates, equivalence classes and
run metadata from the Salmon output directories \code{paths} (named by sample
ID, in any order), computes the tables below and stores them in the
object, so that they are written by \code{\link[=write_isoformic]{write_isoformic()}}. When \code{group} is
given it also stores the fold-change stability of that contrast.
\item \code{tx_uncertainty(x)}: per-transcript table, for the transcripts whose
draws are in the object (the expressed ones).
\item \code{isoform_ambiguity(x)}: per-pair table of replicate correlations.
\item \code{de_confidence(x, group)}: per-feature table of fold-change stability
and confidence class; with \code{collapse_groups = TRUE}, ambiguous isoforms
are summed into one feature before testing.
\item \code{uncertainty_report(x)}: a list of summary tables for a methods or
results section.
\item \code{uncertainty_methods(x)}: a ready-to-edit paragraph that states the
settings used.
}

The replicates themselves are kept in memory only (in
\code{metadata(x)$inf_reps}) and are not written by \code{\link[=write_isoformic]{write_isoformic()}}; the
tables derived from them are. Draws are kept for the transcripts with an
estimated mean count of at least \code{min_count}; the others are treated as
unexpressed.
}
\details{
The thresholds are heuristics, not universal constants: \code{inf_rv_cutoff}
(0.5), \code{cor_cutoff} (-0.5) and \code{min_agreement} (0.95) are conservative
starting points. With 30 draws, sampling noise alone puts the inferential
relative variance of a well-determined, highly expressed transcript near
0.1, so cutoffs much below 0.5 flag noise; poorly determined isoforms
typically reach values of several units. Report the numbers next to the
classes, and rerun with other cutoffs if the data call for it. The replicates measure the
uncertainty of the quantification only; they do not replace biological
replicates and cannot make a noisy contrast significant.

Fold-changes are computed on counts scaled to a common library size, as
\code{log2(mean_b + pseudocount) - log2(mean_a + pseudocount)} with a pseudocount
of 5 (the \code{swish} convention), and the per-replicate test is a Welch
t-test on the log scale with Benjamini-Hochberg adjustment across features.
This is a screening statistic for stability, not a replacement for the
primary test (\code{swish}, \code{DESeq2}, ...), whose results are shown next to it.
When the sample table (\code{col_data}) has a \code{library_size} column, with the
total estimated count of the full quantification, it is used for the scaling.
Set it when the object holds only a subset of the transcripts, so that the
subset does not change the scaling.
}
\examples{
quant_dirs <- list.files(
  system.file("extdata", "mini_quants", package = "isoformic"),
  full.names = TRUE
)
names(quant_dirs) <- sub("_quant$", "", basename(quant_dirs))
annot <- system.file("extdata", "mini_annotation", package = "isoformic")
col_data <- data.frame(
  sample_id = names(quant_dirs),
  condition = rep(c("control", "treatment"), each = 3)
)
iso <- read_salmon(quant_dirs, annot_path = annot, col_data = col_data)

# With your own data, `read_salmon(dirs, annot, inferential = TRUE)` or
# `add_uncertainty(iso, paths = dirs)` read the replicates that
# `salmon quant --numGibbsSamples 30` wrote; here bundled draws are used
draws <- as_inferential_replicates(read_parquet(
  system.file("extdata", "mini_draws.parquet", package = "isoformic")
))
iso@metadata$inf_reps <- draws
iso <- add_uncertainty(iso, group = "condition")
head(tx_uncertainty(iso))
isoform_ambiguity(iso, ambiguous_only = TRUE)
head(de_confidence(iso))
uncertainty_report(iso)$transcripts

}
